Title = {Effects of improper land-use in increasing flood damages in Karaj river
   floodplain (north central Iran) and damage control strategies.},
Booktitle = {Proceeding of the 9th International Conference on Environmental Science
   and Technology Vol B - Poster Presentations},
Series = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Environmental Science and
   Technology},
Year = {2005},
Pages = {B860-B864},
Note = {9th International Conference on Environmental Science and Technology,
   Rhodes Isl, GREECE, SEP 01-03, 2005},
Organization = {Global Network Environm Sci \& Technol; Univ Aegean, Dept Environm
   Studies},
Abstract = {Floodplains are flat land areas which are part of a river system and
   usually experience floods during heavy rain falls. One of the most
   important floodplains in northern Iran, close to the Capital city of
   Tehran is ``Karaj{''} floodplain which is produced by the Karaj River.
   This floodplain is of great importance not only geographically but also
   in terms of industrial activities, population growth and density and
   socio-economically. Due to its low water level during the dry seasons,
   and lack of governmental control, the river banks and peripheries have
   been heavily used for urban and industrial developments.
   Since the river is flowing in a flat floodplain, construction of levees
   and dams or any other structure along the river in order to prevent
   flood damage might be ineffective and requires great expenses and can
   not be justified. Therefore, other means of flood damage prevention
   (i.e., non-structural) must be planned.
   In the present paper, ``SARJOOY{''} village, a newly urbanized area in
   Karaj River floodplain, was chosen as a case study. Floodplain mapping
   was conducted using HEC-RAS hydraulic model. The results were processed
   in GIS environment using ArcView GIs, and hazardous and sensitive zones
   were determined. Outputs of HEC-RAS used for calculating water surface
   levels in urbanized area and two types of residential structures (with
   and without basement) in 100-yr flood event boundaries were chosen for
   damage calculations. By using floodplain regulations in developed
   countries and choosing flood insurance as an indicator and calculating
   the expected costs and insurance rates for each criteria and expected
   annual damage (EAD) for each structural type, guidelines for managing
   this floodplain have been compared and the best method for management is
   proposed.},
