   INDOOR AIR QUALITY AND CLIMATE, VOLS 1-5},
Year = {2005},
Pages = {708-711},
Note = {10th International Conference on Indoor Air Quality and Climate (Indoor
   Air 2005), Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, SEP 04-09, 2005},
Organization = {Tsinghua Univ; Minist Educ China; Natl Nat Sci Fdn China; Beijing
   Municipal Commiss Sci \& Technol; Shanghai Municipal Commiss Sci \&
   Technol; US EPA; Amer Ind Hyg Assoc; Amer Soc Heating, Refrigerating \&
   Air Conditioning Engineers; Int Commiss Occupat Hlth, SC Indoor Air Qual
   \& Hlth; Int Council Res \& Innovat Bldg \& Construct; European Commiss
   Asia Pro Eco Programme Promot Sustainable Solut Environm Europe \& Asia;
   Korean Soc Indoor Environm; Soc Indoor Environm},
Abstract = {Objective: To investigate the association between ventilation and the
   worker's nonspecific building-related symptoms (BRS) in one office.
   Methods: The: study office was located in the sixth floor of one
   semiconductor company with a floor area of 1452 m(2) and a HVAC system
   with sealed windows. A total of 121 out of 174 office workers in this
   office participated in this study. All study subjects completed
   self-administered questionnaires of BRS symptoms during July 2003. The
   participant's perceptions of the office's physical environment were
   obtained in the survey. chi(2) test was used to analyze the prevalence
   of BRS between female and male workers. Multiple logistic regressions
   were used to evaluate the relationships between subjects' BRS symptoms
   and their demographic characteristics and perceptions of the
   environment.
   Results: Eye irritation, nonspecific and muscle symptoms were the most
   prevalent BRS among all the participants with 46\%, 41\% and 25\%.
   Female and allergic workers had significantly higher BRS than other
   workers. BRS was positively associated with worker's' perception of
   inadequate wind velocity.
   Conclusions: Our results showed that the prevalence of office worker's
   BRS was significantly related to their perceptions of inadequate wind
   velocity in office environment.},
