Abstract = {Creating intelligent organizations requires that designers and managers
   rely on the intelligence of individuals to sustain the organization.
   However, individuals are not blank slates without beliefs, emotions, and
   memory processes. The content of their memory and their retrieval
   processes may produce dysfunctional outcomes when incorporated into
   organizational routines. This paper proposes that cognitive therapies
   may help organizational managers and researchers understand how
   intelligent organizations become ``crazy{''} and how to overcome
   disturbed thinking.},
