Pages = {275-283},
Note = {8th International Conference on Landscape and Urban Horticulture, ELECTR
   NETWORK, DEC 15-17, 2021},
Organization = {Int Soc Hort Sci, Div Landscape \& Urban Hort; Int Soc Hort Sci, Div
   Protected Cultivat \& Soilless Culture; Int Soc Hort Sci, Working Grp
   Urban Hort; Univ Catania, Dept Agr, Food \& Environm; Natl Res Council,
   Res Inst Terrestrial Ecosystems; Int Soc Hort Sci, Working Grp Landscape
   Hort},
Abstract = {Opencast mining operations are unavoidably intrusive as the topography
   is altered and the original ecosystems are removed, creating a severe
   impact on the landscape and environment. Depending on the type of mine,
   the scale of the operation, and the method applied opencast mine
   reclamation is often challenging, especially when following a
   sustainable approach. The proposed reclamation of a typical example of
   lignite extraction generated derelict land within the Mediterranean,
   located at Amyntaio in Western Macedonia, Greece, is presented. The
   development of recreational green space is proposed based on a detailed
   site analysis with emphasis given on the socio-economic impact of the
   lignite mine in the region and expressed through the concept ``Earth
   produces energy - from lignite to renewables{''}. The topography of the
   mine is generally preserved; spoil tips are vegetated, and the main pit
   converted to a lake and planted on the periphery with emergent plants
   referring to the process of lignite formation. On the other hand,
   agricultural land is reintroduced with the cultivation of energy crops
   and other small-scale green energy modules are developed within the
   site. By considering the socio-economic impacts of the lignite mine the
   site's ``landscape history{''} is enhanced and the memories of the local
   people preserved.},
