Note = {Forum Belem I on a 3rd Millennium for Humanity - The Search for Paths of
   Sustainable Development, BELEM DO PARA, BRAZIL, NOV 26-29, 1996},
Abstract = {The search for sustainable development is an important goal for the
   survival of humanity. In many areas of human activities, however, the
   situation is problematic. A major obstacle is the lack of participation
   of all citizens in the decision-making. If this keystone is missing,
   people can hardly express their need for a clean and healthy
   environment. Even in countries with well-developed democratic
   traditions, the public has to be vigilant since economic interests may
   counteract a sustainable development. This becomes obvious when looking
   at the management of public forests in the United States. These
   resources have always been considered as superabundant. In 1905, action
   was necessary to prevent the nation's forests from complete destruction.
   The U.S. Forest Service was established to take care of the public
   forests. But, the mandates which were given to the Forest Service were
   conflicting: preservation and commercialisation. The timber industry
   influenced the Forest Service strongly. By 1964, environmentalists
   became so distrustful of the Forest Service's ability to serve the
   broader public interests that the Congress established the National
   Wilderness System, placing the designated primitive areas in
   congressionally protected status. The timber industry continued to lobby
   its interests. In 1993, the Timber Salvage Act was signed allowing the
   logging of large parts of protected areas, although a strong
   environmental impact was predicted. The struggle is not over as logging
   now concentrates in Canada, where the environmental protection by law is
   less developed. Nevertheless, there is some progress towards
   sustainability in the United States. With regard to pollution and land
   preservation, some efforts have been achieved. Emissions of some air
   pollutants such as carbon monoxide, sulphur dioxide and lead have
   dropped. Water pollution of the Great Lakes by persistent organic
   pollutants began to decline. The wounds of nature, however, are still
   not healed. There are further examples from grass root initiatives to
   reduce littering up to ecolabelling to bring about a higher level of
   environmental performance in the marketplace.},
Publisher = {PETER LANG PUBLISHING INC},
