Note = {6th International Conference on Acidic Deposition, TSUKUBA, JAPAN, DEC
   10-16, 2000},
Abstract = {SO42-, NO3 and H+ depositions are estimated in the Brazilian territory
   based on the existing rainfall chemical data arid on annual rainfall
   distribution over the whole territory. Local and regional depositions
   are estimated. Rainfall chemical data over the Brazilian territory shows
   that the average pH values are usually low (between 4.0 and 5.5). These
   values are observed in the tropical Amazon forest as well as in urban
   areas. However, the rainwater acidity in the tropical forests are due to
   organic acids naturally produced by the vegetation while in urban areas
   the acidity is mainly due to acidic anion deposition (NO3- and SO42-).
   In some Amazonian areas, the average input values through rainfall for
   NO3- is about 0.06 keq.ha.yr(-1) and for SO42- is between 0.23 and 0.54
   keq.ha(-1).yr(-1). On the other hand, in some urban centers, such as Sao
   Paulo, values of .072 keq.ha(-1).yr(-1) for NO3- and 1.16
   keq.ha(-1).yr(-1) of SO42- are found and in sites where sulfate sources
   (coal mining) are present, as for the area of Florianopolis, values as
   high as 5.59 keq.ha(-1). yr(-1) for SO42- are found.},
