   Brookhaven Sci Associates; ERIN Engn \& Res Inc; PLG Inc; Sci Applicat
   Int Corp; SCIENTECH INC; Univ Maryland, Ctr Technol Risk Stud},
Abstract = {In this paper, a methodology is introduced to determine the
   characteristic travel distance for airborne semi-volatile organic
   compounds. This measure can be used to evaluate the overall health and
   ecological impact of a chemical release into the environment and
   to-determine whether a chemical is a local, regional, or global scale
   problem. The characteristic travel distance is derived from a steady
   state multi-media environmental system. This methodology is appropriate
   for continual, area-source (non-point) emissions. To demonstrate the
   type of results one can obtain using this approach, a case study was
   completed using 2,3,7,8-TCDD. We found that the characteristic travel
   distance for dioxin is 600 km, the same order of magnitude as the
   distance between urban centers, thus starting to explain the global
   nature of this chemical. Defining the spatial range of a chemical will
   give insight on the appropriate scale for regulation. If a chemical has
   a long spatial range it needs to be managed over a large region as
   opposed to managing just local sources.},
