Year = {1999},
Pages = {166-168},
Note = {15th Annual Meeting of the International-Society-for-Psychophysics,
   ARIZONA STATE UNIV, TEMPE, AZ, OCT 21-24, 1999},
Organization = {Int Soc Psychophys; Arizona State Univ, Coll Engn \& Appl Sci; Arizona
   State Univ, Coll Liberal Arts \& Sci; Arizona State Univ, Dept Psychol},
Abstract = {Despite a long history of investigation and abundance of research
   devoted to understand the mechanisms and processes underlying visual
   space perception (size and distance), still there is no consensus on how
   we see and understand the layout of objects in environments around us.
   The central problem in studies of visual space perception is how
   veridical information about size, distance, shape, location, and
   direction of objects can be recovered from a 2-D image of a scene. This
   complexity remains in part- because the stimulus support for the
   determination of visual scale is far from to be stablished (Hochberg,
   1998; Hershenson, 1999). In this theme session several issues concerning
   to visual space perception are discussed, touching on problems of how
   visual space is measured, what stimulus information is critical for
   conveying a sense of visual scale, and giving theoretical accounts of
   the underlying processes.},
