   UT, 2000},
Organization = {Assoc Women Psychol},
Abstract = {This study was designed to examine personal, stimulus, and
   organizational factors that predict the self-labeling of sexual
   harassment. Hypotheses were developed based on the social cognitive
   schema framework, which suggests that the activation of a victim's
   schema of sexual harassment influences self-labeling incidents as sexual
   harassment. Results of a secondary analysis of the 1995 Department of
   Defense Gender Issues dataset generally supported the hypotheses in that
   self-labeling is a multi-faceted process. Several findings were in the
   opposite direction from that predicted ( e. g., perceptions that the
   military was implementing sexual harassment policies were negatively
   associated with self-labeling). Alternative explanations for the
   complexity of the self-labeling process were also examined.},
