   (ICEEP 2012), Hohhot, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUN 23-24, 2012},
Organization = {Inner Mongolia Univ},
Abstract = {Tribromoacetic acid (TBAA), one of the typically recalcitrant and toxic
   chlorine disinfection byproducts (DBPs), is widespread in the drinking
   water and threating human health. The environment-friendly technology,
   UV irradiation, could efficiently destruct TBAA. The present study
   investigated the removal efficiency, photodegradation kinetics, and
   photodegradation mechanism of TBAA under UV irradiation. It revealed
   that the photodegradaton process of TBAA agreed well with the
   pseudo-first order kinetics, with the rate constant of 1.084 min(-1) and
   half-time of 0.64 minutes. Further study on intermediate products
   formation and mass balance indicated that complete debromination and
   detoxification were obtained with almost all the bromine atoms released
   as bromide ions, and suggest that the cleavage of three C-Br bonds
   concurrently occurred during the photolysis of one TBAA molecule. It
   will provide some guidance for efficient treatment of brominated
   contaminants in water.},
