Abstract = {In this paper coal samples were collected to study fluorine spatial
   distribution character in Chinese coals. The most part of
   Permo-Carboniferous and Jurassic coals in North China are low-fluorine
   coals (<200 mg/kg), accounting for more than 90\% of Chinese coals.
   High-fluorine coals (400mg/kg <= Fc<800mg/kg) and super high-fluorine
   coals (Fc >= 800mg/kg) include stone coal in igneous rock in
   geosynclines region South Qinling Mountain and Late Permian coals in
   South China, but not all Late Permian coals in South China are
   high-fluorine coals. Most stone coals of Early Palaeozoic are
   fluorine-rich coals and local people directly use them as fuel for
   cooking and for warmth in winter, which have resulted in serious indoor
   air pollution and human health problems. But stone coals are not
   included in Chinese coal reserves, so most Chinese coals are
   low-fluorine coals.},
