   Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, DEC 15-17, 2010},
Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc},
Abstract = {Using natural ventilation for space cooling can conserve energy and
   improve indoor air quality. In Hong Kong, the continuous growth of the
   total building stock and the rise of energy consumption in the
   residential sector are receiving greater attention. The public and the
   researchers advocate to wider use of natural ventilation in the
   residential sector. The use natural ventilation in residential premises
   is affected by both the external and the internal factors. The external
   factors include the location, the orientation, the forms of the
   building, the prevailing wind speeds and the surrounding buildings,
   which are subject to constraints beyond the control of site planners and
   architects. Whilst for internal factors like the openings configurations
   and the window types, site planners and architects are always given free
   hand for a proper design. Research on the influence of each of the
   internal factors on the natural ventilation performance is therefore of
   interests to most designers. As one of the series of studies on natural
   ventilation, this paper presents a field measurement study on the
   influences of different opening configurations on ventilation
   performance. Field measurements by tracer-gas method were conducted at a
   case study residential unit in Hong Kong. The CO2 concentration decay
   processes for eight case scenarios were recorded and the mean air age at
   the living room and the bedroom were calculated and compared. The
   studied results provide useful information for future design of
   residential buildings for effective utilization of natural ventilation
   and also can be used to validate CFD simulations for further studies.},
